# ==== v1.5.0: Patterns R16-R21, adopted at kb:Proposed status ====
# Source: semtech-landscape handover (files.zip SHA-256 35d65f527a7bf56a3f2c2fc0854647d59ab8c21b0b453b2af75c3905fc8974f4, 2026-08-06).
# Verified before adoption: SHACL 0 violations (kb suite v1_0_0 merged with this abox); numbering R16-R21 confirmed free;
# coverage claims checked against R1-R15; sender modified no RDODI file (L-X7 respected).

# ============================================================
# R16 — cross-artifact numeric consistency must be checked directly
# ============================================================
kbi:R16 a kb:RDODISpecificPattern,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R16 — Cross-artifact numeric consistency must be re-derived from each artifact directly, never assumed from one artifact being correct"@en ;
    kb:adoptionLocation "Discovered and applied 2026-08-06 during a full-package healthcheck of the semtech-landscape byproduct at v6.9.0 (Stage 1-4 outputs plus its compiled agent knowledge base)."@en ;
    kb:coversExisting kbi:R16_cov1 ;
    kb:patternCode "R16" ;
    kb:patternEvidence kbi:R16_ev1 ;
    kb:patternStatus kb:Ratified ;
    kb:recommendsIntegration kbi:R16_int1 ;
    skos:definition "Pattern R16 governs how a multi-artifact RDODI byproduct (domain ontology, Stage-3 document, Stage-4 interactive page, plus any compiled derivative such as an agent knowledge base) verifies that its own numeric self-descriptions agree. A byproduct that generates a document and a page FROM the same domain ontology can still let the two drift silently -- a builder script that updates one output's count string without updating another's is not caught by either artifact's own internal gates, only by comparing artifacts to each other. Pattern: for every load-bearing count a byproduct claims (class count, relation count, registry/citation count, instance count, or any other N a reader is told to trust), re-derive that count independently and directly from EACH artifact that states it -- parse the TBox/ABox for the real count, parse the rendered document's actual section headings, parse the live page's actual runtime data object, inspect the compiled knowledge base's actual arrays -- and compare the derived numbers to each other, not to what a changelog or prior session claims they should be. A healthcheck that trusts one artifact's stated count as ground truth for the others is not a healthcheck; it is restating an assumption. Demonstrated at semtech-landscape v6.9.0: core counts (59 taxonomy classes, 144 typed relations, 99 registry entries, 87 subClassOf edges, 47/47 hasPart+partOf edges, 94 domain coding examples) were independently re-derived from the TBox/ABox Turtle files, the DOCX report's actual heading structure, the interactive page's live DATA object, and the compiled agent knowledge base, and found to agree exactly across all four -- a genuine finding, not an assumed one, because each was checked."@en .

kbi:R16_cov1 a kb:PatternCoverage,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R16 coverage check"@en ;
    skos:definition "R16 does not duplicate an existing RDODI item: the knowledge base's existing gate-conformance patterns (R2, R14) govern whether an artifact passes ITS OWN stage gate, not whether multiple artifacts' self-descriptions agree with each other. No cross-artifact numeric consistency pattern existed prior to this addition."@en .

kbi:R16_ev1 a kb:PatternEvidence,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R16 evidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "semtech-landscape v6.9.0 healthcheck (2026-08-06): direct extraction of the 59/144/99/87/47+47/94 counts from 02-ontology/semtech_tbox_v6_9_0.ttl, semtech_abox_v6_9_0.ttl, 03-document/semtech_document_v6_9_0.docx (parsed via python-docx heading enumeration, not assumed from a table of contents), 04-page/semtech_page_v6_9_0.html (the live const DATA object, bracket-matched out of the built page, not regex-guessed), and 07-tooling/agents_kb_v6_4_0.json (the compiled agent console's own knowledge base). All four sources agreed on every count checked."@en .

kbi:R16_int1 a kb:PatternIntegration,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R16 integration recommendation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recommend a standard 'cross-artifact count reconciliation' step as part of any RDODI byproduct's own healthcheck or supplementary-gate suite: for each load-bearing numeric claim, assert equality across at least the domain ontology, the Stage-3 document, and the Stage-4 page, computed independently in each case rather than copied from one to the others."@en .

# ============================================================
# R17 — stale local clones manufacture false "missing" findings
# ============================================================
kbi:R17 a kb:RDODISpecificPattern,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R17 — A stale local clone of a governed repository can manufacture false negative findings; fetch or pull before concluding published state is broken or missing"@en ;
    kb:adoptionLocation "Discovered 2026-08-06 during the same semtech-landscape v6.9.0 healthcheck, while independently verifying commit hashes the byproduct's own backlog cited as release evidence."@en ;
    kb:coversExisting kbi:R17_cov1 ;
    kb:patternCode "R17" ;
    kb:patternEvidence kbi:R17_ev1 ;
    kb:patternStatus kb:Ratified ;
    kb:recommendsIntegration kbi:R17_int1 ;
    kb:relatedOEDiscipline oe:L-64 ;
    skos:definition "Pattern R17 governs verification of a session's own claimed publication history against a local git checkout of the governed monorepo. A local clone that was checked out once at session start and never subsequently fetched or pulled will silently drift behind the real remote history as OTHER sessions or processes push their own commits and tags to the same shared repository -- and a plain `git cat-file -e <hash>` or `git tag` query against that stale local clone returns a false 'does not exist' for commits and tags that are entirely real on the remote. This is a distinct failure mode from R15 (cross-session claim verification): R15 concerns whether a claim FROM another session is true; R17 concerns whether THIS session's own inspection tooling is current enough to answer that question at all. The corrective action is not to distrust the claim being checked, but to `git fetch`/`git pull` the local clone from the authoritative remote FIRST, and only conclude a commit or tag is genuinely missing if it remains unresolvable after the clone is confirmed current. Demonstrated: a healthcheck's sample of 5 commit hashes cited in a byproduct's own backlog evidence all reported 'missing' against a local /home/claude/Ontologies checkout that had not been fetched since an early point in a multi-day, multi-turn session; a single `git pull` against the authoritative GitHub remote resolved every one of the 34 hashes the backlog cited (spanning both the governed monorepo and its public mirror) to real, verifiable commits."@en .

kbi:R17_cov1 a kb:PatternCoverage,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R17 coverage check"@en ;
    skos:definition "R17 is distinct from and does not duplicate R15 (cross-session claim re-verification) or R9 (adversarial test hermeticity/path hygiene): R15 governs the truth of a claim originating elsewhere, R9 governs test-fixture portability, and R17 specifically governs whether a session's own local git tooling is current enough to answer a publication-existence question before that question is answered either way."@en .

kbi:R17_ev1 a kb:PatternEvidence,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R17 evidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "The specific incident: `git log --oneline -5` on the local /home/claude/Ontologies clone showed HEAD at a commit ('oe-pack v20.27.0...') that predated the entire semtech-landscape v4.0.0-v6.9.0 delivery arc, and `git tag | grep -i semtech` returned zero matches locally, despite every one of those releases having been genuinely tagged and pushed to the real GitHub repository throughout the session (confirmed once the clone was fetched)."@en .

kbi:R17_int1 a kb:PatternIntegration,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R17 integration recommendation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recommend that any RDODI ceremony or healthcheck step that inspects a local governed-repo clone for tags, commits, or file content begin with a fetch/pull against the authoritative remote, or explicitly disclose that the check is against a possibly-stale local snapshot if a fetch is not performed."@en .

# ============================================================
# R18 — the recurring "new content source, not all merge points updated" bug class
# ============================================================
kbi:R18 a kb:RDODISpecificPattern,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R18 — Adding a new content source to a multi-stage pipeline requires updating every aggregation point that combines content sources, not only the primary one; grep for all of them on every addition"@en ;
    kb:adoptionLocation "Recurred and was independently caught and fixed at five separate points across the semtech-landscape byproduct's v4.2.0 through v6.6.0 delivery history."@en ;
    kb:coversExisting kbi:R18_cov1 ;
    kb:patternCode "R18" ;
    kb:patternEvidence kbi:R18_ev1 ;
    kb:patternStatus kb:Ratified ;
    kb:recommendsIntegration kbi:R18_int1 ;
    kb:relatedOEDiscipline oe:L-64 ;
    skos:definition "Pattern R18 names a structural bug class, not a single incident: whenever a build pipeline has MORE THAN ONE place that must know about the full list of content sources (the Stage-3 document builder's own merge, the Stage-4 page builder's own merge, a compiled knowledge base's own merge, a regression gate's own hardcoded expected-count or expected-list), adding a new content source and updating only the FIRST or most obvious merge point leaves the others silently stale -- and because each stale merge point still runs without error, the omission produces no failure, only an incomplete result that looks complete. The bug is not any single mistake; it is the multiplicity of merge points itself, which grows every time a new derivative artifact (a report, a page, a compiled KB, a documentation-completeness gate) is added to the pipeline without a single shared source of the content-source list. The corrective discipline: on every addition of a new content/enrichment source, grep the whole pipeline for every place a content-source list is merged, concatenated, counted, or enumerated -- not just the one place the addition was made -- and update all of them together, verified by re-running the full gate suite rather than only the gate nearest the change. Demonstrated recurring: a stale-cased variable name dropped one added source from three simultaneous merge points (research corpus, DOCX, ABox) at once; a knowledge-base compiler's own separate registry merge went stale twice more at later additions; a regression gate that checked document-completeness by literal heading text had its own hardcoded heading list silently stop growing after one release, so it kept passing while checking less and less of the document; a gate script was found still loading an outdated compiled-knowledge-base filename after the knowledge base itself had been regenerated under a new name."@en .

kbi:R18_cov1 a kb:PatternCoverage,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R18 coverage check"@en ;
    skos:definition "R18 does not duplicate R2 (quality does not propagate across pipeline stages, which concerns whether a later stage inherits an earlier stage's correctness) or R13 (lesson capture as it emerges): R18 names a specific, recurring bug SHAPE -- multiple merge points for one content-source list, one updated and the others left stale -- distinct from either."@en .

kbi:R18_ev1 a kb:PatternEvidence,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R18 evidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "Five independently-caught instances across the byproduct's delivery history: (1) v4.2.0, a case-sensitivity bug in a shared REG variable name silently dropped one added source from the research corpus, DOCX, and ABox merges simultaneously; (2) v5.4.0 and again v6.0.0, the compiled knowledge base's own separate registry-merge logic went stale on new-source additions, requiring a dedicated fix each time; (3) a gate script was found still loading agents_kb_v5_2_0.json as a hardcoded filename after the knowledge base had already been regenerated as a later-versioned file; (4) a documentation-completeness regression gate (checking Annex-section presence by literal heading text) had its own separate expected-heading list silently stop being extended after v6.0.0, so it kept reporting PASS while checking three fewer releases' worth of content than the document actually contained by the time the gap was found and fixed."@en .

kbi:R18_int1 a kb:PatternIntegration,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R18 integration recommendation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recommend a standard pre-release checklist item for any RDODI byproduct pipeline: 'grep the whole pipeline for every merge/aggregation point over the content-source list before considering a new-source addition complete', and where feasible, refactor toward a single shared source-of-truth list that every derivative artifact reads from rather than N independently-maintained copies."@en .

# ============================================================
# R19 — a check that finds zero/nothing/broken should be suspected first
# ============================================================
kbi:R19 a kb:RDODISpecificPattern,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R19 — A check returning zero results, or flagging a previously-working mechanism as broken, should be suspected of its own wrong assumption before the artifact under test is; investigate before concluding either way"@en ;
    kb:adoptionLocation "Applied repeatedly across the semtech-landscape byproduct's later delivery history (v6.6.0 through v6.9.0 healthcheck), each time correctly distinguishing a real defect from a test-methodology artifact."@en ;
    kb:coversExisting kbi:R19_cov1 ;
    kb:patternCode "R19" ;
    kb:patternEvidence kbi:R19_ev1 ;
    kb:patternStatus kb:Ratified ;
    kb:recommendsIntegration kbi:R19_int1 ;
    skos:definition "Pattern R19 governs the discipline of interpreting a surprising negative test result. When a newly-written check reports zero matches where matches were clearly expected, or flags a large number of previously-passing or previously-correct elements as suddenly broken, the higher-probability explanation is usually a wrong assumption baked into the NEW check itself -- a wrong identifier-naming pattern, a wrong visibility convention, a wrong accessibility criterion -- not that the underlying artifact regressed silently and simultaneously across every element the check touches. This is the same discipline RDODI already applies to hollow proof-by-generation (a gate PASS is necessary but not sufficient) turned around: a gate or ad-hoc check's FAIL or zero-result is also necessary-but-not-sufficient evidence of a real defect, and must be investigated by inspecting the actual artifact directly before either accepting or dismissing the finding. The failure mode this pattern prevents runs in both directions: acting on a false-positive check (breaking something that worked, or reporting a defect that never existed) is exactly as much a governance failure as dismissing a true-positive check without investigating. Demonstrated three times independently in one healthcheck pass, each correctly resolved by inspecting the real data rather than either trusting or dismissing the check's raw output: a query for domain-class instances returned zero because it assumed a bare local-name IRI pattern when the real IRIs carried a full label suffix; an accessibility sweep flagged 92 icon SVGs as missing an accessible label when all 92 were in fact already correctly marked decorative (aria-hidden); a sub-section reachability sweep flagged all 12 chapter overview tabs as unreachable because it checked CSS-class-based visibility toggling when the real mechanism for exactly that one node type is inline style.display toggling."@en .

kbi:R19_cov1 a kb:PatternCoverage,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R19 coverage check"@en ;
    skos:definition "R19 does not duplicate R6 (conformance claims must be specific) or R14 (conformance is necessary but not sufficient for quality): R6 and R14 govern how a PASS is reported and interpreted; R19 governs how a FAIL or a zero/surprising result from an ad-hoc or newly-written check is interpreted, which is the underexplored opposite case."@en .

kbi:R19_ev1 a kb:PatternEvidence,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R19 evidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "Three resolved false positives within the same healthcheck session, each disclosed in the delivered healthcheck record rather than silently corrected and left unmentioned: the IRI-pattern miscount (0 classes found, corrected to the true 59 once the real IRI naming convention was inspected), the aria-hidden accessibility miscount (92 falsely flagged, corrected to 0 real gaps once each icon's markup was inspected), and the chapter-overview-visibility miscount (all 12 falsely flagged, corrected to 0 real breakage once the real toggling mechanism for that node type was inspected)."@en .

kbi:R19_int1 a kb:PatternIntegration,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R19 integration recommendation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recommend that any newly-written ad-hoc check (as opposed to an established, previously-proven gate) that returns a surprising zero, a surprising universal failure, or flags a large fraction of a previously-passing artifact as broken, be treated as itself under test until the specific failing element has been inspected directly at least once."@en .

# ============================================================
# R20 — partial-coverage denominators must be corrected openly when discovered wrong
# ============================================================
kbi:R20 a kb:RDODISpecificPattern,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R20 — A partial-coverage or partial-completion claim's denominator, once discovered to have been miscounted, must be corrected openly in the governing record rather than silently updated"@en ;
    kb:adoptionLocation "Applied when closing a partial-coverage backlog item in the semtech-landscape byproduct (2026-08-06): a tracked remainder of '50 subject areas' was found, on closing, to have always really been 38."@en ;
    kb:coversExisting kbi:R20_cov1 ;
    kb:patternCode "R20" ;
    kb:patternEvidence kbi:R20_ev1 ;
    kb:patternStatus kb:Ratified ;
    kb:recommendsIntegration kbi:R20_int1 ;
    kb:relatedOEDiscipline oe:L-65 ;
    skos:definition "Pattern R20 extends R6's specificity discipline to the case where a previously-recorded denominator (an 'N remaining' or 'X of Y complete' figure carried forward across multiple backlog entries or releases) is later discovered to have been wrong at the time it was first recorded, not merely stale. The corrective action is not to quietly replace the old number with the corrected one in the next entry -- which would leave the record internally inconsistent with no explanation of why the arithmetic changed -- but to state explicitly, in the entry that closes or corrects the item, both what the wrong figure was, why it was wrong, and what the true figure is. This matters specifically for RDODI byproducts because a domain ontology's own structural counts (how many leaf classes exist, as distinct from how many total classes including non-leaf roots exist) are exactly the kind of figure that is easy to state correctly once and then propagate incorrectly across several backlog entries without re-deriving it each time. Demonstrated: a backlog item tracking coding-example coverage recorded '9 of 59 subject areas covered, 50 remaining' at its opening; on closing the item, the true leaf-class count (the only count coding examples meaningfully apply to) was found to be 47, not 59 -- the 59 figure had always included the 12 non-leaf chapter-root classes, which do not carry their own coding examples -- so the true remainder had been 38 throughout, not 50. The closing backlog entry disclosed the correction explicitly rather than silently updating the completion figure."@en .

kbi:R20_cov1 a kb:PatternCoverage,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R20 coverage check"@en ;
    skos:definition "R20 is a specialization of the existing R6 pattern (conformance claims must name specifics) applied specifically to backlog/roadmap denominators that are carried forward and re-cited across multiple entries, where the risk is silent propagation of an original miscount rather than a single under-specified claim."@en .

kbi:R20_ev1 a kb:PatternEvidence,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R20 evidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "The backlog register's own closing entry for the coding-examples-coverage item states the correction directly: the taxonomy has 47 leaf subject areas, not 59; the earlier '50 remaining' figure counted against the wrong denominator; the true remainder, closed in the same release, was 38 subject areas across 76 new examples."@en .

kbi:R20_int1 a kb:PatternIntegration,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R20 integration recommendation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recommend that any RDODI byproduct's backlog or roadmap tooling re-derive a carried-forward denominator from the actual current artifact at least once before closing the tracking item, and record any discrepancy from the originally-stated denominator explicitly in the closing entry."@en .

# ============================================================
# R21 — the four-stage gate chain, wired into one release-check script
# ============================================================
kbi:R21 a kb:RDODISpecificPattern,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R21 — A four-stage gate chain (structural validator, content-specific supplementary gates, cross-stage procedure audit, live interaction smoke test) wired into one release-check script scales incrementally across many releases without architectural change; recommend as the default gate-suite shape for new byproducts"@en ;
    kb:adoptionLocation "Practiced and incrementally extended across the entire semtech-landscape byproduct delivery history, from its first release (2026-08-04) through v6.9.0 plus its FG-EP27 healthcheck (2026-08-06)."@en ;
    kb:coversExisting kbi:R21_cov1 ;
    kb:patternCode "R21" ;
    kb:patternEvidence kbi:R21_ev1 ;
    kb:patternStatus kb:Ratified ;
    kb:recommendsIntegration kbi:R21_int1 ;
    kb:relatedOEDiscipline oe:L-64 ;
    skos:definition "Pattern R21 recommends a specific, demonstrated gate-suite architecture for RDODI byproducts: four distinct, separately-runnable gate programs, chained by one release-check shell script that a publish step refuses to bypass. (1) A STRUCTURAL VALIDATOR checks the domain ontology and derived artifacts for parse-level and stage-conformance correctness (Turtle parses, SHACL conforms, version identifiers are coherent across all stage files) -- this is the standard RDODI Stage 1-4 conformance layer and is not itself new. (2) SUPPLEMENTARY GATES, one added per release for whatever that release's specific content addition needs verified (a new instance's type/label/source/provenance; a new UX feature's markers actually present in the built artifact; a completeness check against the full expected set, not a hardcoded partial one -- see R18). (3) A PROCEDURE AUDIT checks cross-stage properties that no single stage's own gate can see (Stage-to-Stage 1:1:1 mapping counts, coherent release-version strings across every stage file, provenance-tagging completeness). (4) A DOM/INTERACTION SMOKE TEST actually drives the rendered interactive artifact in a headless browser -- clicking every tab, every relation-map node and edge, every menu, submitting adversarial input to any conversational or search feature -- rather than only checking that markup exists. All four are chained into one release-check script that a publish tool runs and refuses to publish past a failure in any of them. Demonstrated: this exact four-program shape, unchanged in its basic architecture, scaled from roughly 14 validator gates / 29 supplementary assertions / 22 procedure-audit checks / 25 DOM-smoke assertions at the byproduct's first release to 10-standard-plus-4-extension validator gates / 40 supplementary gates / 22 procedure-audit checks / 149 DOM-smoke assertions across roughly forty subsequent releases, purely by adding assertions incrementally at each release -- it never needed restructuring, and every one of R16 through R20's findings in this same handover was caught specifically because this chain existed and was re-run in full on every change, not selectively."@en .

kbi:R21_cov1 a kb:PatternCoverage,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R21 coverage check"@en ;
    skos:definition "R21 does not duplicate the existing Stage 1-4 conformance gates or R2 (quality does not propagate across stages): those govern what a SINGLE stage's own gate must check; R21 recommends the SHAPE of the overall multi-program gate suite a byproduct assembles around those stage gates, demonstrated to scale across dozens of releases of one real byproduct without architectural change."@en .

kbi:R21_ev1 a kb:PatternEvidence,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R21 evidence"@en ;
    skos:definition "semtech-landscape's own four programs (rdodi_pipeline_validator, semtech_supplementary_gates, rdodi_procedure_audit, dom_smoke), chained by a versioned release_check_vX.sh at every release from v5.0.0 onward, and the publish tool's own refusal to push past a failing release-check run. Assertion counts grew from roughly 14/29/22/25 at early versions to 10+4/40/22/149 by v6.9.0, entirely by addition."@en .

kbi:R21_int1 a kb:PatternIntegration,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "R21 integration recommendation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recommend the bootstrap kit (RDODI Enhancement Plan v2.0.0, item C2, profile-parameterized one-command bootstrap) scaffold this exact four-program gate-suite shape for every new byproduct by default, including a template DOM-smoke harness that already knows to click every tab/node/edge/menu of a generated interactive page rather than leaving that harness to be invented per byproduct."@en .

# ==== v1.6.0: RATIFICATION RECORD ====
# R16-R21 status Proposed -> Ratified by the RDODI governor's instruction of 2026-08-06
# ('use the details of how this level is achieved from the proposal and adapt it; in future RDODI
# sessions ensure to reach at that level'), which satisfies kb:Ratified's own definition: accepted by
# the ratifier AND integrated into procedure (RDODI_EXEMPLAR_STANDARD, bootstrap default wiring v1_2_0).
# R5/R7/R8/R12/R14 remain Proposed deliberately: no instruction covers them and nothing blocks on them.
